Driving as Side Task Exploring Intuitive Input Modalities for Multitasking in Automated Vehicles Henrik Detjen henrik.detjen@hs-ruhrwest.de Univ. of Appl. Sciences Ruhr West Bottrop, Germany Stefan Geisler stefan.geisler@hs-ruhrwest.de Univ. of Appl. Sciences Ruhr West Bottrop, Germany Stefan Schneegass stefan.schneegass@uni-due.de University of Duisburg-Essen Essen, Germany ABSTRACT Through vehicle automation, the human is more and more able to execute non-driving-related activities (NDRAs). However, con- trol interfaces are still needed to allow human intervention and foster a sense of control. This paper presents a driving simulator experiment ( N = 20 ) with five likely NDRAs (being idle, eating, smartphone use, conversation, listen to music) where participants freely intervened in the driving process, using either touch, voice, or mid-air gesture interaction. We found that NDRAs determine input modality choices. Generally, participants tended to avoid the modalities demanded by the NDRAs, e.g., voice interaction becomes less frequent while listening to music. Contrary, touch interaction increased during smartphone use, indicating that users tend to stick to a known interaction style during high multitasking workload. Overall, we recommend designing future vehicle interiors and inter- faces that rely on multimodal interfaces to account for the diversity of situations and activities while driving automated. CCS CONCEPTS • Human-centered computing → Human computer interac- tion (HCI) ; User studies ; Interaction techniques . KEYWORDS Automated Vehicles, Non-Driving-Related Activities, Driving-Related Activities, Maneuver-based Interventions, Control, Multitasking, Multimodal Interaction, Touch, Voice, Mid-Air Gestures. ACM Reference Format: Henrik Detjen, Stefan Geisler, and Stefan Schneegass. 2021. Driving as Side Task: Exploring Intuitive Input Modalities for Multitasking in Automated Vehicles. In CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems Extended Abstracts (CHI ’21 Extended Abstracts), May 8–13, 2021, Yokohama, Japan. ACM, New York, NY, USA, 6 pages. https://doi.org/10.1145/3411763.3451803 1 INTRODUCTION Automated cars will change the role of the driver. Traditionally the driver is responsible for the driving task. With increasing techno- logical progression and the integration of driving assistant systems into cars, the driving task shifts more and more to the system. Permission to make digital or hard copies of all or part of this work for personal or classroom use is granted without fee provided that copies are not made or distributed for profit or commercial advantage and that copies bear this notice and the full citation on the first page. Copyrights for components of this work owned by others than the author(s) must be honored. Abstracting with credit is permitted. To copy otherwise, or republish, to post on servers or to redistribute to lists, requires prior specific permission and/or a fee. Request permissions from permissions@acm.org. CHI ’21 Extended Abstracts, May 8–13, 2021, Yokohama, Japan © 2021 Copyright held by the owner/author(s). Publication rights licensed to ACM. ACM ISBN 978-1-4503-8095-9/21/05. . . $15.00 https://doi.org/10.1145/3411763.3451803 Reaching certain degrees of automation (cf., SAE level 3–5 [22]), the system can complete the driving task itself, at least for a cer- tain time, depending on the level. From then on, the human driver becomes the passenger (e.g., from entering until exiting the high- way) and is free to engage in non-driving-related activities (abbr.: NDRAs): one can do work, entertainment, or social activities during travel. Nevertheless, even in autonomous driving phases, human- vehicle interaction is still necessary to provide mechanisms to the human driver to intervene in the driving task, e.g., to adapt driv- ing parameters, take cooperative maneuvers, or foster a sense of control. The NDRAs highly influence the context of such driving- related activities (abbr.: DRAs). When the occupants want to in- tervene in the driving process, they must shift their attention to the new task, and the current activity must be interrupted. Inter- ference between NDRA and DRA because of similar requirements for spatial/verbal cognitive resources (cf. [25]), might lead to per- formance breakdowns. Thus, it is crucial to understand the inter- play between NDRA and DRAs in automated vehicles. While the definition [19], prediction [5, 12, 13, 18], and automated recogni- tion [17] of NDRAs, as well as their influence on take-over request performance [6, 10, 15, 20, 27] have been studied recently, their influence in less critical multitasking scenarios is still unexplored. This paper therefore explores the influence of highly anticipated non-driving-related activities (main task) on the driver/passenger’s modality choices when intervening in the driving process (side task) in a driving simulator experiment ( N = 20 ). To interact in a subjectively natural and comfortable way, participants could freely choose between touch, voice, and mid-air gesture modalities. We were interested in resulting patterns of interaction and possible differences in multitasking performance. Thereby, we hypothesize that (1) NDRAs would influence the chosen input modalities for control interventions, and (2) the selected modality would be differ- ent from the modality used for the main task. Our study helps to understand better which interaction modalities are used naturally in the context of different NDRAs. Further, it is a first step towards the activity-optimized development of in-car HMIs for driving-related control interventions in future automated cars. 2 RELATED WORK In the following, we distinguish DRAs from NDRAs and show how control in automated cars will change. The growing automation will lead to new activities, and new activities will change the user requirements for in-car interfaces. 2.1 Non-Driving-Related Activities In conventional cars, one can divide the driving task into three categories (cf. Bubb [1]): 1) transportation-related tasks which are
