CHI ’21 Extended Abstracts, May 8–13, 2021, Yokohama, Japan Detjen, Geisler & Schneegass required to reach a destination (e.g., use the steering wheel), 2) transportation-related tasks which are optional to reach a par- ticular destination (e.g., use the windscreen wiper), and 3) non- transportation-related tasks (e.g., use media control). With the system’s growing responsibility for the primary and secondary driving tasks through automation, the former driver becomes more and more a passenger in automated cars, and ter- tiary tasks become more important than before. To simplify the categorization of tasks in automated vehicles, we use the terms Driving-Related Activities or Tasks and Non-Driving-Related Activ- ities or Tasks as proposed by Pfleging & Schmidt [19]. Expected NDRAs are, for example [5, 12, 13, 18]: Watching out of the win- dow, talking to other passengers, using the smartphone, eating, listen to music/radio, taking a nap, knitting, or office work. These activities require different physical and cognitive resources and influence the human vehicle user’s capabilities and preferences to control the car. 2.2 Control During Autonomous Driving An essential requirement of cooperative driving is directability [24]. From a functional perspective, use cases where the human might want to intervene in an automated ride are, for example [23]: Influ- encing the route taken, adjusting the driving style of the vehicle (e.g., to accelerate more quickly at the traffic light), or overtaking of slow traffic before the highway exit. From a psychological per- spective, it is beneficial to provide a control interface possibility. Compared to being passive in autonomous driving, the possibility to exercise control increases driving pleasure and attractiveness [9] and the acceptance of the system [16]. Traditional controls (steering wheel and pedals) might not be the best option because they require detailed awareness of the driving situation on the operational level, e.g., the exact steering angle for a DRA. A more comfortable way of controlling the car could be maneuver-based or trajectory-based control [7] because operational aspects of the driving task remain with the vehicle. We refer to these concepts as Trajetory-based Interventions and Maneuver-based Interventions (abbr.: MBI). Such cooperative concepts combine the advantage of automated driving with user needs and requirements. Because these driving interventions are intentional actions of the driver/passenger, interfaces for maneuver control commonly rely on explicit and fast interaction modalities, e.g., touch [8, 14], voice, or mid-air gestures [3, 4]. However, objective utility and subjec- tive perception of these interfaces are heavily dependent on the interaction context and, thus, on the NDRAs. 2.3 Multitasking and Multimodal Interaction During autonomous travel times, vehicle occupants are likely to perform NDRAs. When they are occupied with a particular NDRA, they spend physical and mental resources for that task, e.g., read- ing a book requires the physical effort to hold the book and the mental effort to decode and understand the written letters – eyes and hands, as well as attention, are dedicated to this task. Control interventions potentially use the same and thus might interfere with these resources. Wickens [26] defines four dimensions of re- source sharing in multitasking settings: 1) processing dimension (perception, cognition, response), 2) codes of processing dimen- sion (spatial vs verbal), 3) modalities dimension (auditory vs visual perception), and 4) visual channels (focal vs ambient). The less a multitasking procedure occupies the same dimensions, the better the later performance. Roider et al. [21] found for input modality switches for side tasks during driving, that transitions between interaction modali- ties can increase efficiency compared to unimodal conditions and that switching modalities does not increase overall task comple- tion time. Further, their results indicate that distractions from the main task are determined by the used modality of the side task, not by transition processes. Once distraction from the main task (driving) is no longer the primary concern of interaction, the sys- tem should promote modality switches between tasks so that users could benefit from individual modality’s efficiency and suitability for a particular task. Thus, it might be useful to switch control intervention modality during an NDRA, e.g., voice control would not be the best choice while listening to music, and touch not the best choice while using the smartphone. 3 EXPERIMENT We conducted a within-subject driving simulator experiment to explore modality choices for control interventions in multitasking scenarios. On a short test track, participants performed five possi- ble non-driving-related activities (counterbalanced order) during which they intervened four times in the driving process. Each time they could choose between either voice, touch, or mid-air gestures (resulting in 20 choices per participant or 80 choices per condition). Sample. We conducted the study at the University of Applied Sci- ences Ruhr West. Twenty computer science students ( m = 17; f = 3 ) with an average age of 22.45 years ( SD = 4 . 55; Min = 18; Max = 33 ) participated. Task A: Driving-Related Activity. We told participants that they would ride a safe driving car (cf. driving phases during SAE level 3–5) operating in a cooperative driving mode that requires their con- trol. We instructed participants to adhere to the following routine during the rides, intervening in the autonomous driving process: A1) start the vehicle ( start maneuver ), A2) command the car to drive onto the motorway from the rest area ( lane change maneuver ), A3) control the vehicle to leave the highway at the next rest area ( lane change maneuver ), and A4) park at a rest area ( park maneuver ). Participants were free to choose an interaction modality out of voice, touch, and mid-air gestures. We selected these modalities because they allow for direct interaction. For touch interaction, we used a maneuver-board interface on a touchpad (identical to [14]), and for voice and mid-air gesture interfaces, we used a user-defined command set (identical to [3]). Task B: Non-Driving-Related Activity. Based on studies on ex- pected NDRAs (cf. [5, 12, 18]), we discussed frequent activities concerning their potential impact on driving-related interventions. Afterward, we selected five prototypical activities for our exper- iment that would involve participants in different ways. For the selected tasks, we gave participants the following instructions in addition to Task A (cf. Table 1). Setup and Wizard of Oz. We used a driving simulator (Software: SILAB 6.0) with sounds for the study (see Figure 1). Sounds, e.g.,
